 =========================================================
   Rounding Elements using border-radius
   =========================================================

   border-radius is a CSS property used to round the corners
   of an element. It affects the outer edges of the element,
   including the background and border.

   HOW IT WORKS:
   - The value controls how curved the corners are
   - Larger values create more rounded corners
   - Smaller values create slightly rounded corners

   COMMON VALUES:
   - border-radius: 4px;
     → Subtle rounding (inputs, forms)

   - border-radius: 8px or 12px;
     → Smooth rounded corners (cards, containers)

   - border-radius: 50%;
     → Creates a perfect circle when width and height are equal

   INDIVIDUAL CORNER CONTROL:
   border-radius follows this order:
   top-left → top-right → bottom-right → bottom-left

   Example:
   border-radius: 10px 20px 30px 40px;

   IMPORTANT POINTS:
   - Works on block and inline-block elements
   - Border and background are both rounded
   - Shape depends on element width and height
   - Commonly used for buttons, cards, images, and inputs

   EASY RULE:
   Bigger value → more curve
   50% → circle
   =========================================================
